# Cloudflare Pages response headers.
#
# Cloudflare reads this file from the ROOT OF THE BUILD OUTPUT, so it lives in
# public/ and Astro copies it to dist/ verbatim. It is not served as a file.
#
# ⚠️ EVERYTHING IN THIS FILE FOLLOWS THE SITE INTO PRODUCTION. That is the whole
# difference from netlify.toml, which sat at the repo root and was never copied
# into dist/ — so it could carry preview-only rules safely and this file cannot.
#
# So this file holds ONLY rules that are correct on the real procedoinfo.com as
# well as on the preview. The preview's `X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow` is NOT
# here, and must never be added here: it would follow the build into production
# and quietly de-index the real launch. It is appended to dist/_headers at
# deploy time by scripts/preview-headers.cjs, which `npm run build:preview`
# runs and `npm run build` does not.
#
# The same trap is why public/robots.txt carries no Disallow — see the note in
# that file.

# Added 2026-09-19 after auditing what the live site actually sent: nosniff and
# Referrer-Policy and nothing else. For a firm whose own services page sells
# Facilities Security and "Enterprise-Grade Security", a prospect who runs the
# site through securityheaders.com or Mozilla Observatory is a plausible
# visitor — and the grade was the kind you do not want them to see. The
# technical risk on a static brochure site with no auth and no user content is
# genuinely low; the credibility risk is not.
#
# Each of these denies something the site does not do, so none can break it:
#   HSTS                 the site is HTTPS-only already; this stops a first
#                        request being made over http. No `preload` — that is a
#                        one-way door for a domain and is Procedo's call, not a
#                        default to slip in.
#   X-Frame-Options      nothing should ever frame this site.
#   Permissions-Policy   no camera, microphone or geolocation is used anywhere.
#   COOP                 no cross-origin window is opened or talked to.
#
# THE CSP BELOW CARRIES NO HASHES, WHICH IS WHY IT CAN LIVE IN A STATIC FILE.
# A hash-locked policy was built twice — once by hand, once with Astro's own
# security.csp — and both broke the site. astro.config.mjs records the full
# finding; the short version is that Astro's ClientRouter neuters already-run
# scripts with a `data:application/javascript,` URL, which no hash and not even
# strict-dynamic will allow, and Astro's styleDirective always adds hashes,
# which per spec makes 'unsafe-inline' be ignored and blocks all 64
# `style="--reveal-delay:Nms"` attributes.
#
# WHAT THIS POLICY STILL BUYS, on a site with no login, no database and no
# user-generated content anywhere:
#   · no script may load from any other origin — a compromised dependency or a
#     hijacked CDN link cannot pull one in
#   · no <object>, <embed> or <applet>, and no framing of this site at all
#   · no <base> tag rewriting where relative URLs resolve to
#   · forms can POST to this origin and Web3Forms and NOWHERE else, so a
#     submitted enquiry cannot be redirected to an attacker
#   · XHR/fetch is limited to the same two places
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT BUY: it cannot stop INLINE script injection, because
# 'unsafe-inline' has to stay. That matters far less here than it would
# elsewhere — every page is static HTML built from src/data/site.ts, and there
# is no input anywhere that is echoed back into a page, so there is no route by
# which an attacker could get markup onto it in the first place. If that ever
# changes — a comment form, a search page that reflects its query, anything
# rendering user input — this policy stops being adequate and the inline styles
# and the router question have to be solved properly.
#
# connect-src and form-action name Web3Forms because the contact form fetches
# https://api.web3forms.com/submit, and falls back to a native POST to the same
# URL when JavaScript is unavailable.
#
# THE TWO CLOUDFLAREINSIGHTS ORIGINS WERE ADDED 2026-09-27, WITH ANALYTICS, AND
# NOT BEFORE. They are the whole reason this policy needed touching: the beacon
# is a third-party script, which is exactly what `script-src 'self'` exists to
# refuse. Measured rather than predicted — with analytics switched on at the
# Pages project and this policy unchanged, every page load logged
#
#   Loading the script 'https://static.cloudflareinsights.com/beacon.min.js'
#   violates the following Content Security Policy directive: "script-src
#   'self' 'unsafe-inline' data:". The action has been blocked.
#
# so nothing was measured AND every page gained a console error, against
# FINISH-LINE's recorded zero. Two entries are needed, not one: the script
# itself loads from static.cloudflareinsights.com, and it POSTs its beacon to
# cloudflareinsights.com/cdn-cgi/rum, which connect-src governs. Allowing only
# the first gets a script that loads and then silently cannot report — the
# worse failure, because nothing in the console says so.
#
# If analytics is ever switched off again, TAKE THESE BACK OUT. A policy that
# permits an origin the site no longer uses is a policy that has stopped
# describing the site.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; base-uri 'self'; object-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; frame-src 'none'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' data: https://static.cloudflareinsights.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self'; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://api.web3forms.com https://cloudflareinsights.com; form-action 'self' https://api.web3forms.com; upgrade-insecure-requests

# Astro fingerprints these filenames, so they can be cached forever.
/_astro/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# ── The static images, which are NOT fingerprinted ──────────────────────────
# Added 2026-09-27. Until now `/_astro/*` was the only Cache-Control rule in
# this file, so everything under public/ was served with none at all and the
# header logo — which loads on every page of the site — was revalidated on every
# navigation. Found by Lighthouse; it is the cheapest repeat-visit win here.
#
# ⚠️ THESE CANNOT BE `immutable`, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE REASON THEY NEED THEIR
# OWN RULE. Astro fingerprints the filenames under /_astro/, so a changed file
# is a different URL and a one-year immutable cache is safe. These filenames are
# fixed: replace the brand artwork and `procedo-logo-header.png` still points at
# whatever a visitor already has. `immutable` here would mean a rebrand that
# never reaches anyone who had visited before, with no way to force it short of
# renaming the file.
#
# One day is the compromise: a repeat visitor inside the same day pays nothing,
# and new artwork reaches everyone within 24 hours without a deploy trick.
# `stale-while-revalidate` lets the week after that serve instantly and refresh
# in the background, which is the right trade for a logo — a day-old mark is not
# a defect, a blocking request for one is.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400, stale-while-revalidate=604800

/favicon.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400, stale-while-revalidate=604800

# Only social crawlers ever fetch this — it is referenced from a <meta> tag and
# no visitor downloads it — but the rule costs nothing and a crawler re-fetching
# 111 KB on every scrape is pure waste.
/og-default.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400, stale-while-revalidate=604800
